class.description:
[VideoQualitySettingsServer] is the settings domain [code]"video_quality"[/code]: it stores the 3D scaling ([member Viewport.scaling_3d_mode], [member Viewport.scaling_3d_scale], [member Viewport.fsr_sharpness]), the mesh LOD threshold ([member Viewport.mesh_lod_threshold]), the anti-aliasing ([member Viewport.msaa_2d], [member Viewport.msaa_3d], [member Viewport.screen_space_aa], [member Viewport.use_taa], [member Viewport.use_debanding]), variable rate shading ([member Viewport.vrs_mode]) and occlusion culling ([member Viewport.use_occlusion_culling], [method RenderingServer.viewport_set_occlusion_rays_per_thread], [method RenderingServer.viewport_set_occlusion_culling_build_quality]) for the player. Every value takes effect at once on the root viewport. The settings resource is a [VideoQualitySettings]; the file is [code]user://settings/video_quality.ini[/code] by default, in the format of [member ProjectSettings.settings/storage/format].
When the game starts, the stored values are applied in [constant SettingsServer.APPLY_PHASE_SCENE], after the root viewport exists and before the main scene is loaded. Values set earlier wait until then. The defaults are the project settings ([member ProjectSettings.rendering/scaling_3d/mode], [member ProjectSettings.rendering/anti_aliasing/quality/msaa_3d], [member ProjectSettings.rendering/mesh_lod/lod_change/threshold_pixels], [member ProjectSettings.rendering/vrs/mode], [member ProjectSettings.rendering/occlusion_culling/use_occlusion_culling] and the others), with their feature overrides such as [code].mobile[/code].
[b]Further viewports:[/b] the per-viewport values also go to the viewports added with [method add_viewport] and to every [Viewport] in the group [method get_viewport_group], for example a [SubViewport] that renders the 3D world of a pixel-art game.
[b]Renderers:[/b] the Compatibility renderer (default in the Web export) only scales with Bilinear or Nearest and has no FSR, FXAA/SMAA, TAA, debanding or variable rate shading. The Mobile renderer has no FSR and no TAA. Variable rate shading needs GPU support, MetalFX needs Metal (macOS, iOS), occlusion culling needs the [code]raycast[/code] module. [method SettingsServer.is_value_supported] returns [code]false[/code] for such values and [method get_unsupported_setting_names] lists them, so that a settings menu can gray them out. Such values are still stored, but not pushed; a warning names them once. A 3D scaling mode the renderer can't use is stored, and bilinear scaling is used.
Values out of range are clamped with a warning: [code]scaling_3d_scale[/code] [code]0.1[/code] to [code]2.0[/code], [code]fsr_sharpness[/code] [code]0[/code] to [code]2[/code], [code]mesh_lod_threshold[/code] [code]0[/code] to [code]1024[/code], [code]occlusion_rays_per_thread[/code] [code]1[/code] to [code]2048[/code]. An enum value out of range is replaced by the default.
[codeblocks]
[gdscript]
func _ready():
	$RenderScale.value = VideoQualitySettingsServer.get_scaling_3d_scale()
	$Msaa.selected = VideoQualitySettingsServer.get_msaa_3d()
	$Taa.disabled = not VideoQualitySettingsServer.is_value_supported(&"use_taa")

func _on_render_scale_value_changed(value):
	VideoQualitySettingsServer.set_scaling_3d_scale(value)

func _on_msaa_item_selected(index):
	VideoQualitySettingsServer.set_msaa_3d(index as Viewport.MSAA)
[/gdscript]
[csharp]
public override void _Ready()
{
    GetNode<HSlider>("RenderScale").Value = VideoQualitySettingsServer.GetScaling3DScale();
    GetNode<OptionButton>("Msaa").Selected = (int)VideoQualitySettingsServer.GetMsaa3D();
    // Methods of the base class are called through Singleton.
    GetNode<CheckBox>("Taa").Disabled = !VideoQualitySettingsServer.Singleton.IsValueSupported("use_taa");
}

private void OnMsaaItemSelected(long index)
{
    VideoQualitySettingsServer.SetMsaa3D((Viewport.Msaa)index);
}
[/csharp]
[/codeblocks]
[b]Note:[/b] In C#, the static class [code skip-lint]VideoQualitySettingsServer[/code] only has the methods of this class. The methods of [SettingsServer] are called through [code]VideoQualitySettingsServer.Singleton[/code].
[b]Note:[/b] A game that sets these properties on the root viewport itself overwrites the player's values until they are applied again. The domain is never applied to the editor itself.
